---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Spidergraph — Digital Legacy Worksheet"
  description="A local-only worksheet for who should reach your accounts in an emergency, without ever writing down a live password. Nothing here leaves your browser."
  path="digital-legacy-worksheet/"
  current="digital-legacy"
  accent="amber"
>
  <link
    slot="head"
    rel="stylesheet"
    href={`${base}pages/digital-legacy-worksheet.css`}
  />

  <div class="wrap">
    <header>
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">
          Worksheet · Domain 11: Legal &amp; Documentation
        </div>
        <h1>
          Digital Legacy <span>Worksheet</span>
        </h1>
        <div class="sub">
          Get a trusted person oriented before you need them to be — where
          things are, who to contact, and how to reach your accounts without you
          ever writing down a live password.
        </div>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="reset-btn" id="resetBtn">
        Reset worksheet
      </button>
    </header>

    <div class="intro-card">
      <b>Why this exists:</b> Domain 11 of the Playbook recommends "a written,
      offline document listing account recovery info location for a trusted
      person, without exposing live credentials." This turns that recommendation
      into something you actually fill out. It is <b>not</b> a will, a legal
      document, or a substitute for an estate attorney — it's a working note.
      <div class="warn">
        <b>Never write an actual password here.</b> Use your password manager's
        built-in emergency access feature (Bitwarden and Proton Pass both
        support a trusted-contact request-and-wait-period model) so a trusted
        person can get in without a live credential ever existing in writing.
      </div>
      <div class="links">
        <a href="../playbook/#11-legal-documentation">
          📖 Read Domain 11 in the Playbook →
        </a>
        <a href="../privacy-stack-builder/">
          ⚙️ Compare password managers with emergency access →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="layout">
      <div>
        <div class="form-card">
          <div class="form-card-title">Trusted contact</div>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="contactName">
              Who should be contacted in an emergency?
            </label>
            <input
              type="text"
              id="contactName"
              placeholder='Name or relationship (e.g. "my sister, Jamie")'
              maxlength="120"
            />
          </div>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="contactMethod">
              How will they know to act, and how do you reach them?
            </label>
            <textarea
              id="contactMethod"
              placeholder="e.g. a note in my will names them; their number is saved in my phone under..."
              maxlength="500"
            ></textarea>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="form-card">
          <div class="form-card-title">Where recovery info lives</div>
          <div class="field">
            <label for="recoveryLocation">
              Physical or logical location of recovery materials
            </label>
            <textarea
              id="recoveryLocation"
              placeholder="e.g. fireproof document safe in the hall closet; safe deposit box at [bank], key location..."
              maxlength="500"
            ></textarea>
            <span class="hint">
              Recovery codes, a printed list of accounts, attorney contact info
              — the location, not the credentials themselves.
            </span>
          </div>
          <div class="field">
            <label>Password manager emergency access</label>
            <div class="radio-group" id="emergencyAccessGroup">
              <label class="radio-option">
                <input type="radio" name="ea" value="not-set-up" />
                <span>
                  <span class="opt-label">Not set up yet</span>
                  <span class="opt-desc">
                    The single highest-leverage step this worksheet points to —
                    see the Stack Builder link above.
                  </span>
                </span>
              </label>
              <label class="radio-option">
                <input type="radio" name="ea" value="set-up-untested" />
                <span>
                  <span class="opt-label">Set up, not tested</span>
                  <span class="opt-desc">
                    Configured in Bitwarden/Proton Pass but never actually
                    verified end-to-end.
                  </span>
                </span>
              </label>
              <label class="radio-option">
                <input type="radio" name="ea" value="set-up-tested" />
                <span>
                  <span class="opt-label">Set up and tested</span>
                  <span class="opt-desc">
                    Your trusted contact has actually confirmed they can request
                    and receive access.
                  </span>
                </span>
              </label>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="form-card">
          <div class="form-card-title">
            Accounts to flag for your trusted contact
          </div>
          <div class="field" style="margin-bottom:10px;">
            <span class="hint" style="margin-top:0;">
              Labels only — email, financial, password manager, etc. Never an
              actual username, password, or account number.
            </span>
          </div>
          <div id="acctList" class="acct-list"></div>
          <div id="noAccounts" class="no-accounts" hidden>
            No accounts flagged yet.
          </div>
          <div class="add-row">
            <input
              type="text"
              id="acctInput"
              placeholder="e.g. Primary email (Proton), Password manager (Bitwarden)"
              aria-label="Account label to flag"
              maxlength="120"
            />
            <button type="button" id="acctAddBtn">
              Add
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="form-card">
          <div class="form-card-title">Additional notes</div>
          <div class="field">
            <textarea
              id="notes"
              placeholder="Anything else your trusted contact would need to know — attorney contact, business/creator entity details, anything from Domain 11 that applies to you."
              maxlength="1000"
              style="min-height:100px;"
            ></textarea>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sidebar">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Completeness</div>
          <div class="score-row">
            <div>
              <div class="score-num" id="scoreNum">
                0/6
              </div>
              <div class="score-label">sections filled in</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="save-indicator" id="saveIndicator"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Companion tool</div>
          <p class="small">
            The other half of "what happens if something goes wrong" is the
            Incident Response Card — the numbers you call, not the person who
            calls them for you.
          </p>
          <a class="card-link" href="../incident-response-card/">
            Incident Response Card →
          </a>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">Export</div>
          <button type="button" id="exportBtn" class="reset-btn">
            Export worksheet ↓
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-title">History</div>
          <p class="small">
            Save a dated checkpoint to compare completeness over time.
          </p>
          <button
            type="button"
            id="snapshotBtn"
            class="reset-btn"
            style="width:100%; margin-bottom:12px;"
          >
            Save snapshot
          </button>
          <div id="historyList" class="history-list"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <footer>
      <b>Companion pages:</b> the "why" behind this worksheet is Domain 11 of
      the
      <b>Playbook</b>. Everything you type here stays in this browser only, in{' '}
      <code>localStorage</code> — nothing is sent anywhere, ever, and this page
      never asks for or stores an actual password. <b>Reset worksheet</b> clears
      everything.
    </footer>
  </div>

  <script src={`${base}shared/persist.js`} is:inline></script>
  <script src={`${base}shared/history.js`} is:inline></script>
  <script src={`${base}pages/digital-legacy-worksheet.js`} is:inline></script>
</BaseLayout>
